# Vercel Web Analytics Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for the youth-pos-web project.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` package (version 1.5.0)
- Updated `package.json` to include the new dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` with the latest dependency tree

### 2. Analytics Integration
**Modified:** `src/app/layout.tsx`
- Added import statement: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next"`
- Added `<Analytics />` component inside the `<body>` tag following Next.js App Router best practices
- Preserved existing code structure and formatting

## Implementation Details

Following the official Vercel documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart), the Analytics component was integrated into the root layout file for Next.js App Router:

```typescript
import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next";

export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <html lang="id">
      <body>
        {children}
        <Analytics />
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

This placement ensures that analytics tracking is active on all pages of the application.

## Verification

✅ **Build Status:** Successful - The Next.js build completed without errors
✅ **Linting:** No new linting errors introduced by the changes
✅ **Type Checking:** TypeScript compilation successful

## Next Steps

After deployment to Vercel:
1. Enable Analytics in the Vercel dashboard for this project
2. Verify tracking is working by checking the browser's Network tab for `/<unique-path>/view` requests
3. Monitor analytics data in the Vercel dashboard

## Notes

- The Analytics component is automatically tree-shaken in development mode and only active in production
- No additional configuration is required for basic page view tracking
- The integration follows Next.js 16.x and React 19.x best practices
